# A pitch to every possible reader (`Whether you're a beginner or an
# expert, the CLI fits your workflow`) names no reader in particular,
# so the fix names who the feature is for or drops the clause.
#
# `whether you are a` also opens a real condition (`the check reports
# whether you are a member of the group`), so the pitch needs the `or`
# that pairs two kinds of reader, up to three words after the article.
# Each gap length is its own alternative, since a list picks its
# longest match and never backtracks. `whether you're new to` counts
# without the `or`, because a condition rarely asks it.
expr you [you're, (you are)]
expr main [(whether @you [a, an] * or), (whether @you [a, an] * * or), (whether @you [a, an] * * * or), (whether @you new to)]
let message "Vague audience. Name who the feature is for, or delete the clause."
let description "Flags a clause that pitches to every kind of reader, such as whether you're a beginner or an expert."
let becomes ""
let kind "Style"
allows "The check reports whether you are a member of the group."
